FINDINGS = [
 ("F1","Background","Chapter 19 of the 3rd edition teaches how a program keeps time, waits, schedules and launches other programs: the time module with the epoch timestamp of time.time(), the text of time.ctime(), profiling by subtracting two timestamps, and time.sleep(); the Super Stopwatch project; the datetime module with datetime objects, fromtimestamp(), comparison, the timedelta type, arithmetic on dates, pausing until a date, and strftime() and strptime() with the directives of Table 19-1; subprocess.run() and subprocess.Popen() with poll(), wait() and kill(), command line arguments, captured output, and opening a file with the start or open command; a pointer to Task Scheduler, launchd and cron; the Simple Countdown project; seven practice questions and two practice programs, the Prettified Stopwatch and the Friday the 13th Finder. The book's chapter ontology models six sections and seven practice questions but not the two projects.",["P01"]),
 ("F2","Comparative analysis","The chapter's date and duration arithmetic reproduces under Python 3.14.4: a timedelta of 11 days, 10 hours, 9 minutes and 8 seconds has days 11, seconds 36548 and microseconds 0, total_seconds() 986948.0 and the text 11 days, 10:09:08; 1,000 days after 2 December 2026 at 18:38:50.636181 is 28 August 2029; 21 October 2026 minus 30 times 365 days is 28 October 1996 and minus 60 times that is 5 November 1966; the three strftime() formats and the strptime() parses print what the chapter shows, and the comparisons of the three Halloween and New Year datetimes give the chapter's True, False, True, True.",["P01","P03"]),
 ("F3","Comparative analysis","Several printed outputs of the chapter cannot be reproduced as printed because they depend on the time zone of the author's computer: fromtimestamp(1000000) prints 5:46:40 on 12 January 1970, which is the moment in a zone eight hours behind UTC, while the same call gives 13:46:40 in UTC and 15:46:40 on a machine at UTC plus three; time.ctime() formats the local time on a 24-hour clock, so a reading at 11:04 PM prints 23:04 and not the 11:05:38 the chapter shows; and the chapter's timestamp 1773813875 is 23:04:35 on 17 March 2026 in Pacific Daylight Time (UTC minus 7), although the chapter says Pacific Standard Time.",["P01","P02","P03"]),
 ("F4","Comparative analysis","The chapter's rule for two-digit years is wrong by one: the text and Table 19-1 say %y spans 1970 to 2069, but the documentation and the interpreter map the values 69 to 99 to 1969 to 1999 and the values 0 to 68 to 2000 to 2068, so strptime() reads 69 as 1969 and 68 as 2068; the chapter's own examples, 63 as 2063 and 73 as 1973, are right under either rule.",["P01","P02","P03"]),
 ("F5","Comparative analysis","Four listings of the chapter as served on its web page contain the typographic en dash in place of the minus sign (end_time – start_time, time.time() – last_time twice, and oct_21st – about_thirty_years), which Python rejects with a SyntaxError that names the character, U+2013; the Windows example that opens hello.txt writes 'C:\\\\Users\\Al\\\\hello.txt' with an unescaped backslash before the A, which current Python compiles with a SyntaxWarning about an invalid escape sequence, the warning that has existed since Python 3.12 and is planned to become an error; and the prose calls datetime.timedelta() datetime.delta(), a name the module does not have.",["P01","P11"]),
 ("F6","Comparative analysis","The chapter profiles code by subtracting two time.time() readings, and the documentation adds why that is fragile: time.time() reads the system clock, which can be set back so that a later call returns a smaller value, while time.monotonic() and time.perf_counter() are monotonic clocks that cannot go backwards and are not affected by clock updates, so only a difference of two readings is meaningful and the difference is the right measure of a duration; the chapter's calcProd.py also forms a product with 1,516,705 bits, and printing it fails under the default limit of 4300 digits for integer to text conversion that Python has had since 3.11, a limit the chapter does not meet because it prints only the elapsed time.",["P01","P02","P10","P13"]),
 ("F7","Comparative analysis","The chapter's claim that time.sleep() pauses a program for the given number of seconds is sharpened by the documentation: since Python 3.5 a sleep interrupted by a signal is restarted, so it lasts at least the requested time, but it may last longer by an arbitrary amount because of scheduling; since 3.11 it uses clock_nanosleep() or nanosleep() on Unix; and the chapter's loop that sleeps one second and compares datetime.now() with a target compares naive local times, which a change of the computer's clock or its daylight saving rules moves, so a long wait is better handed to the operating system's scheduler, as the chapter itself advises.",["P01","P02","P03","P14"]),
 ("F8","Contemporary developments","Current Python deprecates the datetime call the old idiom relied on and gives the tools the chapter leaves out: datetime.utcnow() and utcfromtimestamp() have raised a DeprecationWarning since 3.12 in favour of datetime.now(datetime.UTC); a datetime is naive or aware according to its tzinfo, a naive and an aware value are never equal and cannot be ordered; the zoneinfo module supplies IANA zones; adding a timedelta to an aware datetime in one zone moves the wall-clock time, so one day added across the start of daylight saving time is 23 hours of elapsed time; and the fold attribute of PEP 495 tells apart the two passes through a repeated hour.",["P03","P07","P11","P15","P16"]),
 ("F9","Contemporary developments","Parsing a month and a day without a year has changed: since Python 3.13 strptime() emits a DeprecationWarning because the default year 1900 is not a leap year, so '02 29' fails, and the documentation says such formats may raise an error as of Python 3.15; the remedy is to include a year, and Python 3.14 adds date.strptime() and time.strptime() beside datetime.strptime().",["P03","P12"]),
 ("F10","Comparative analysis","The chapter's subprocess account holds and current Python adds to it: run() waits and returns a CompletedProcess whose text form lists the arguments and the return code and, when output was captured, the captured text; a program that cannot be found raises FileNotFoundError; check=True turns a nonzero exit code into CalledProcessError and timeout= kills the child and raises TimeoutExpired; a Popen object's poll() returns None while the child runs and the exit code afterwards, kill() ends the child so that its return code is -9 on Linux (a negative value means a signal), and the documentation warns that wait() can deadlock when the child's output goes to a pipe that nobody reads; none of the chapter's Windows and macOS launches of Calculator, Paint and Notepad could be run, because they need those programs and a screen.",["P01","P04"]),
 ("F11","Contemporary developments","The chapter's advice to open a file with start or open and its use of shell=True on Windows meet a security rule of the documentation: a list of arguments is passed to the program without a shell, so spaces and metacharacters are safe, whereas with shell=True the program must quote every piece of text itself, for which shlex.quote() exists on POSIX; text joined into a shell command runs as two commands, and quoted it does not; the webbrowser module's open() and, on Windows only, os.startfile() are the documentation's ways to open a page or a file with its default program, and none of them was run here.",["P01","P04","P08","P09"]),
 ("F12","Contemporary developments","The chapter hands scheduling to Task Scheduler, launchd and cron, which run programs even when no Python program is alive; the standard library offers only in-process scheduling that ends with the program: sched.scheduler runs events in order of time and then priority (a lower number first) with a clock and a delay function that can be replaced, and threading.Timer runs a function after a delay in a thread and can be cancelled before it fires.",["P01","P05","P06"]),
 ("F13","Conclusion","For an advanced course, chapter 19 is best taught as the choice of a clock by the question asked and of a launcher by the question asked: a wall-clock moment is a datetime (aware, in a zone), a duration measured on the machine is a monotonic or performance counter difference, a duration between moments is a timedelta, and a program is launched with a list of arguments through run() or, when it must run alongside, Popen; the chapter's date arithmetic holds under 3.14.4, and the zone dependence of its printed times, the two-digit year range, the en dash and backslash slips, the monotonic clocks, aware datetimes and zoneinfo, the year-less parsing warning, security of shell=True, and in-process scheduling are what the chapter leaves for the course to add.",["P01","P02","P03","P04","P05","P07"]),
]
